# SHEscale — Backend Structure Build Prompt

> **Purpose of this document:** A *prompt* to hand to an AI coding assistant (or backend engineer) to generate the full backend folder/file structure and connect it to the already-built MongoDB models. This is not the code itself — it is the instruction set that produces it.

---

## 🎯 Context to Give the AI

```
You are a senior backend engineer (20+ years experience) building the Node.js/Express
backend for "SHEscale" — a unified 3-pillar platform for female entrepreneurs
(Funding, Mentorship, Networking).

The MongoDB models are ALREADY BUILT and live in backend/models/:
User.model.js, FundingApplication.model.js, GovernmentScheme.model.js,
FinancialPlan.model.js, InterviewSession.model.js, ChatMessage.model.js,
Match.model.js, MentorBooking.model.js, LocalBusiness.model.js,
MicroGrant.model.js, plus models/index.js as the central export point.

You already have full context of the project's feature specification and
architecture document (the SheBuilds AI / SHEscale spec covering all 15 features
across the Funding Hub, Mentorship Hub, and Networking Hub, plus the Live
Government Scheme Intelligence Panel). FOLLOW THAT ARCHITECTURE DOCUMENT
STRICTLY — every route, controller, and service you build must map back to a
feature described in it. Do not invent features or endpoints that aren't in
that spec.
```

---

## 🧩 Master Instruction (paste this to the AI)

```
Build the complete backend folder and file structure for SHEscale on top of the
existing models/ folder shown above. Follow these rules strictly:

1. MODULE SYNTAX: Use ES6 `import` syntax everywhere (e.g. `import express from "express"`).
   Do NOT use CommonJS `require()` anywhere in any file. Set `"type": "module"` in package.json.

2. CODE OPTIMIZATION: Keep every file lean and single-purpose.
   - Controllers only orchestrate (validate input → call service → return response).
   - Business logic (scheme matching, PDF parsing, RAG retrieval, financial calculations,
     matching algorithms) lives in a `services/` layer, never inline in controllers or routes.
   - No duplicated logic across files — extract shared logic into `utils/`.
   - Use async/await with a single centralized error-handling middleware — do not
     repeat try/catch boilerplate in every controller; use an `asyncHandler` wrapper.

3. DATABASE CONNECTION: Create a `config/db.js` that connects to MongoDB using
   Mongoose and the existing models via `models/index.js`. Connection string must
   be read from `.env` (already has `.env.example` — follow its variable names).
   Include connection retry/error logging, and call this connector once from the
   main `server.js` entry point before the app starts listening.

4. FILE & FOLDER STRUCTURE: Generate a clear, updated structure under `backend/`:
   backend/
     config/          → db.js, env loader, third-party API config (Gemini, Calendly, payment gateway)
     models/          → (already exists — do not modify, only import from here)
     routes/          → one route file per resource (auth, funding, scheme, mentorship, networking)
     controllers/      → one controller per route file
     services/         → business logic + the RAG pipeline (see section 5 below)
     middlewares/       → auth (JWT), error handler, file upload (multer), rate limiter, validation
     utils/            → asyncHandler, response formatter, logger, redaction helper for sensitive docs
     jobs/             → scheduled/cron jobs (government scheme sync — see section 5)
     scripts/          → (already exists — reuse for one-off/manual data scripts)
     server.js         → app entry point
     app.js            → Express app config (middleware registration, route mounting)

5. 🆕 RAG PIPELINE FOR THE LIVE GOVERNMENT SCHEME INTELLIGENCE PANEL:
   This is the most important new architecture piece — build it as a proper
   Retrieval-Augmented Generation flow, not a simple API call:
   - `jobs/schemeSyncJob.js` — a scheduled job (node-cron) that periodically fetches
     raw scheme data/PDFs from official government scheme sources and passes each
     one through Gemini's structural-extraction prompt, then upserts the structured
     result into the GovernmentScheme collection (eligibility, financial terms,
     required documents, deadlines, application steps).
   - `services/ragRetrievalService.js` — given a user's natural-language business
     query (from the Conversational Scheme Matcher), retrieve the most relevant
     subset of currently-stored GovernmentScheme documents (by embedding similarity
     or structured filter matching on business type/location/loan size — your call,
     justify the choice), then pass ONLY that retrieved context + the user query
     into a Gemini completion call to produce the final matched-scheme response.
     This keeps matching grounded in live, current scheme data instead of the
     model's own training knowledge.
   - `services/geminiService.js` — a single shared wrapper around all Gemini API
     calls (scheme extraction, PDF Doc-Whisperer parsing, financial plan generation,
     AI Twin Mentor chat) so there is one place that handles prompts, retries, and
     API key config — not scattered fetch calls across services.
   - Expose the retrieval result through a `GET /api/schemes/match` endpoint that
     the frontend's Scheme Matcher feature calls.

6. SECURITY & MIDDLEWARE:
   - JWT-based auth middleware protecting all routes except signup/login.
   - Role-based access control for admin-only endpoints (e.g. manually triggering
     the scheme sync job).
   - Multer-based file upload middleware for document scanning (Feature 1.3),
     with the redaction utility applied before anything touches the DB.
   - Centralized error-handling middleware returning consistent JSON error shapes.
   - Basic rate limiting on auth and AI-calling routes.

7. OUTPUT FORMAT: First output the full folder/file tree, then generate each file's
   code in its own labeled code block (e.g. `backend/config/db.js`), in the order:
   config → server.js/app.js → middlewares → utils → services → jobs → controllers → routes.
   After all files, add a short "Data Flow Summary" explaining how a single request
   (e.g. Conversational Scheme Matcher) travels from route → controller → RAG
   retrieval service → Gemini service → GovernmentScheme model → response.
```

